// What every scene program takes per frame, as one array (uFrame):
//   [0] xyz  towards the light that casts shadows (the sun, the moon); w: haze per metre
//   [1] rgb  light on a face that looks sideways (the mean of sky and ground light) over LIGHT_SCALE; w: how far
//            the city's lights are on
//   [2] rgb  what a face that looks up has more than that, over LIGHT_SCALE
//   [3] x    most haze a point can be under; y: seconds
// The light and the tint travel to the fragments as colours, which the scene's parameter buffer keeps in a few
// bytes; a colour holds 0..1, so the light is carried at 1 / LIGHT_SCALE and the sun's colour (which may pass 1)
// stays a fragment uniform.
// A fragment is lit by the sun when it stands at or above the shadow height of its place (uShadow, in the
// units of the vertices' own heights): fully at that height, not at all 1 / SHADOW_K below it.
// Per draw: uMvp, which also undoes the normalization of the positions, and uMap, the scale (xy) and offset
// (zw) from a vertex's own x and z to the height grid's texture coordinates.

// How much of the haze colour a point takes, by its depth: 1 - exp(-x), near enough.
float haze_of(float depth, float4 frame[4])
{
    float x = depth * frame[0].w;
    return min(1.0 - 1.0 / (1.0 + x + 0.5 * x * x), frame[3].x);
}
